{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789488910,"nonce":"XwFxOZG7xCLdKmKmH8wfAnU/jzI=","title":"The Cranelift ISA is built twice with different flags, and the rebuild ignores the opt-level switch","message":"`CraneliftBackend::new` built its ISA reading `ZYNTAX_OPT_LEVEL` and `ZYNTAX_CRANELIFT_VERIFIER`; `rebuild_with_accumulated_symbols`, which every tiered runtime goes through once the plugins are registered, built another with `speed` and the verifier off, hard-coded. So the switch did nothing in a real run (an A/B of `none` against `speed` came out identical), and the verifier flag differed between a fresh backend and a rebuilt one.\n\nNow one `host_isa()` serves both. The internal verifier (the ISA flag, which verifies between Cranelift's own passes) defaults off, as the rebuilt path had it; `ZYNTAX_CRANELIFT_VERIFIER=1` turns it on. The explicit `verify_function` before compile stays.\n\nWith the switch working, `ZYNTAX_OPT_LEVEL=none` measured on the M1 Pro: Cranelift codegen about 10 to 15% faster, exec op_overload 65 to 86 ms, branchy 90 to 97, collatz 66 to 60, tokenize unchanged. `speed` stays the default.","files":null}]}